# `arvo-strategy`: a name bound to an objective and a selection

**Canon this design declares.** The strategy-axis rows, chiefly
`ruling::the_strategy_set_is_not_closed_at_four`, `ruling::the_strategy_intents_are_not_clear_cut`,
`ruling::each_preset_names_a_stated_intent`, `ruling::the_strategies_weigh_measurements_differently`,
`ruling::cold_is_not_to_be_deprioritised` and
`ruling::there_is_no_exchange_rate_because_there_is_no_generalisation`.
`ruling::the_derivation_is_a_placement_and_the_operation_set_is_an_admission_rule`, for the position of
the selector. `ruling::arms_over_regions_are_the_fundamental_heart` and
`ruling::the_work_is_predicated_arms_composed`, for what this crate supplies to the one that ships them.

## What this crate is

A strategy is a binding from a name to a placement objective and an adaptation selection. It is not
the key of the derivation ladder and it is not a closed enumeration.

The ladder is keyed on the placement objective and the strategy-to-objective map is the selector, so
a strategy name stays a binding rather than becoming a behaviour.

That kills the selector-versus-key disagreement rather than resolving it: the two readings are one
function curried two ways, and the floor removed the thing that made the difference observable. So
this crate ships the map, and `arvo-placement` ships the ladder the map points into.

## The set is open, and this crate is built so it stays open

The set is not closed at exactly four, and nothing in `arvo-strategy` presumes it is. See
`ruling::the_strategy_set_is_not_closed_at_four`.

So the presets are implementors of an open concept, exactly as formats are. A new strategy joins by
supplying the obligations. There is no enumeration to extend and no match to widen.

**Openness is a licence to question the decomposition rather than an instruction to replace it.** The
four names are kept because nothing has found them wrong, and a name that describes its part well is
worth more than a fresh one that describes it as well.

## Why the per-strategy statements are intents and not tables

Each preset's intent is clear and nothing about it is absolute, so the crate does not write them as
though they were settled. See `ruling::the_strategy_intents_are_not_clear_cut`.

A design shipping four markers with a behaviour table against each writes exactly what that forbids.
So the crate carries each preset's intent, in the words the canon has for it, and carries no table of
behaviours keyed on the name.

What the canon does state per preset, kept as intent:

The speed-first preset exists for performance and efficiency even at the cost of accuracy or
soundness, and sacrificing soundness is its explicit purpose rather than a tolerated defect, but the
price is a provable meaningful gain. What counts as meaningful is unset and nobody has set it.

The intuitive-compromise preset does not merely imitate. Its intent is to be the intuitive best choice
for most every use case, and the intuitive part demands it mimics, but mimicry is dropped where it is
consistently the worse choice. Being a Rust crate makes Rust's way the baseline for what a reader
finds intuitive; it is not what makes it correct.

The storage-minimising preset is for cold paths and cold storage and stays small for memory or disk.
It has leeway to be inefficient and is not obliged to use it. It is not deprioritised, and that
survives the set being reshaped, renamed or resized.

The accuracy-first preset sacrifices as much performance and efficiency as makes sense to reach the
most precise answer, especially within chains rather than only per operation. Nothing has measured
that objective yet, so what the preset is for is stated and what it costs is not.

**The crate carries the axis; a concrete value has to fill it, and what fills it is a default rather
than a settled answer.** `presets` ships one `Adaptation` per preset, because a Rust associated type
has to name something, and naming something is not the same as deciding it. Whether `Warm` wraps or
clamps is open, at `question::does_warm_wrap_or_clamp` under `topic::overflow_policy`. `Warm`'s
`Adapt<HalfEven, Wrap>` and the other three presets' choices are marked `// FIXME:` at the
declaration for exactly that reason: carried rather than endorsed, and replaceable by whatever
`does_warm_wrap_or_clamp` and its neighbours in `overflow_policy` and `rounding` settle, with no edit
to `Strategy` itself.

So do not read a preset's adaptation as the answer for that preset. Read it as the value the crate
compiles with until there is one.

## Measurement decides, and the weightings differ

The mechanism under all of the above is that every strategy is decided by measurement, they measure
different things, and they weigh different measurements differently. See
`ruling::the_strategies_weigh_measurements_differently`.

So a strategy is not a rule that short-circuits evidence. It is a weighting over evidence, and an
answer that decided without measurement would be unfalsifiable. That is why the concept this crate
ships is a weighting and a selection rather than a behaviour.

Whether the weightings agree in practice is an ordinary empirical question and the canon owes no
answer to it.

## There is no exchange rate, so the selection is per arm

The question this crate would otherwise have to answer, how much of one objective a unit of another
is worth, is refused rather than answered. See
`ruling::there_is_no_exchange_rate_because_there_is_no_generalisation`. It is decided case by case,
in words that get recorded, and even a small win over a small set is a win worth taking. Nothing here
generalises: const predicates choose the most optimal path, out of many small implementations for
many small situations.

So there is no rate at which one objective yields to another, and there is no ranking in this crate.
Where a conflict between objectives arises, the arm states in words why it resolves the conflict the
way it does, and that justification is recorded beside the arm.

**A million small implementations is the intended shape rather than a failure mode**, which bears on
any later review that would flag the count as duplication.

## What an arm is, and why this crate supplies the predicate rather than the match

An arm is an expression that is optimal over a nameable region, carrying a const predicate over that
region and applying nowhere else. The full space is filled by composing arms, so everywhere is optimal
by construction rather than by one statement uniform across the dimensions. That is the heart of
arvo, at `ruling::arms_over_regions_are_the_fundamental_heart`, and it is not a claim this crate
proves in isolation: `arvo-placement`'s `derive_sole` and
`derive_shared` are the two arms that already exist in the tree, matching on `O::OBJECTIVE`, an
associated const of the objective marker this crate names.

**So the arm is not here.** This crate supplies the const value the arm downstream is gated on
(`Strategy::Objective`, reached through `objective_of`), and it is that value, not a match this crate
writes, that makes `derive_shared`'s branch a predicate rather than a runtime check. A consumer generic
over `S: Strategy` never sees the arm directly; it reaches `arvo_placement::derive_shared::<F,
S::Objective>()`, and the branch there is what selects.

A predicate may read anything available at const time, and may not read anything that is not, because
selection happens at monomorphisation and there is nothing there to read. A condition known only at
runtime is not an arm: it stays a finding about where a law holds, and a consumer who knows their data
satisfies it selects the arm at their own call site from their own const knowledge.

**No arm ships in this crate, and that is not a gap in the ruling.** Building one here, at the
strategy-binding layer rather than the placement layer, would mean varying a strategy's own
`Adaptation` by something other than the strategy name, and nothing in the corpus has measured a
region where that pays. Shipping such a thing anyway would be exactly the unjustified generalisation
`ruling::there_is_no_exchange_rate_because_there_is_no_generalisation` refuses, dressed as a predicate.
The concepts an arm is gated over are what this crate writes; the arm itself waits on a measurement.

## What this crate does not decide

The strategy object's internal component count is `topic::the_strategy_object` and is out of scope, as
are `what_the_cost_coordinates_are` and `is_i9_about_a_strategy_or_one_component`.
`are_strategies_partially_ordered_by_chain_laws`, `must_a_weighting_be_strictly_positive`,
`arms_no_weighting_can_select` and `which_axes_a_build_arm_may_move` are open in the registry and none
is reached by the binding concept written here.

`the_cross_repo_strategy_name_collision` is open and is a naming call, not a mechanism.

## The laws this crate owes tests for

**A strategy names no implementation.** The binding maps to an objective and a selection, and a
contract test asserts that the type of a strategy does not mention a placement or an operation. If
that test cannot be written the separation is not real, and the failure to compile is the finding.

**Adding a preset requires no edit to any existing item.** Asserted by adding one in the test module
and showing everything still resolves. That is what an open inventory means and it is checkable rather
than asserted.

**Two presets over one declared signature agree on every declared operation**, because the strategy
selects the placement and the adaptation, and the placement does not change what is computed. Where
they disagree, the disagreement is in the adaptation, which is a coordinate of the declared signature
and therefore a different signature.

**No behaviour table is keyed on a preset name.** Structural, and the reason is that a per-strategy
statement is an intent rather than a settled behaviour.

**The selection is const.** A predicate that is not const-available does not compile as an arm
selector, and the test asserting that is a compile-fail test rather than a runtime one.

## The surface this design names

`Strategy` is the binding and has exactly two items: `OBJECTIVE`, which is the
`arvo_placement::Objective` the ladder is keyed on, and `Adaptation`, which is the
`arvo_format::Adaptation` this binding selects. Two items and no third, because a
third keyed on the preset's name would be the behaviour table the canon says not to
write.

`objective_of` reaches the first through the binding, so a consumer never has to
know which preset it holds.

**The intents are rustdoc on each preset and are not a trait item.** A preset's
purpose is, in this design's own earlier words, something a reader can read and
nothing a compiler can gate on, and a `const` in a trait is precisely something a
compiler gates on. Carrying it as one also made a per-preset compile-time item
keyed on the preset, which is nearer the behaviour table
`ruling::the_strategy_intents_are_not_clear_cut` forbids than prose is. So the
intent lives where a reader reads it and nowhere the type system can see.

`Hot`, `Cold`, `Precise` and `Warm` are the presets, in `presets`. They are
instances of an open concept rather than members of an enumeration, and a consumer
adding a fifth edits nothing here. **Which overflow mode and rounding mode each
names is a carried default, marked `// FIXME:` at each impl, and not a settled
answer**, because `does_warm_wrap_or_clamp` is open at least for `Warm`, and
filling that in silently inside a design is how an open question gets closed
where nobody can see it happen.
